\section{Introduction}\label{sec:energy-intro}

For most of the history of computing, the primary goal of compilation was to
produce code that runs as \emph{fast} as possible.  Speed was the universal
currency: faster code meant more throughput, lower latency, and better user
experience.  But in the modern era---characterized by mobile devices, data
centers, and the Internet of Things---\emph{energy} has become an equally
important compilation target.

Energy consumption affects every level of computing:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Mobile devices:} Battery life is the primary user-facing metric.
\item \textbf{Data centers:} Energy costs account for 40\% of the total cost of
      ownership of a data center~\cite{barroso2013datacenter}.
\item \textbf{Embedded systems:} Many devices must operate for years on a
      single battery or energy-harvesting source.
\item \textbf{Environmental impact:} Computing accounts for an estimated 2--4\%
      of global carbon emissions~\cite{strubell2020energy}.
\end{itemize}

\emph{Energy-aware compilation} is the practice of optimizing compiled code for
energy efficiency, either as a primary objective or in trade-off with
performance.  This chapter explores the theory and practice of energy-aware
compilation, from energy modeling (\cref{sec:energy-models}) to optimization
techniques (\cref{sec:energy-optimization}) and thermal-aware scheduling
(\cref{sec:energy-thermal}).

