% Energy as a Compilation Target
% ============================================================
\section{Energy as a Compilation Target}\label{sec:energy-target}

\subsection{The Multi-Objective Optimization Problem}\label{sec:energy-multi}

Energy-aware compilation is fundamentally a \emph{multi-objective optimization}
problem: the compiler must balance energy consumption against performance, code
size, and other objectives.

\begin{definition}[Energy-Aware Compilation]\label{def:energy-compile}
Given a program $P$, a target architecture $A$, and an objective function
$f : \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}^k$ mapping compiled code to a vector of metrics
(e.g., $(\mathit{time}, \mathit{energy}, \mathit{size})$), energy-aware
compilation finds $c \in \mathcal{C}$ that minimizes $f(c)$ according to some
preference ordering on $\mathbb{R}^k$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Pareto Optimality}\label{sec:energy-pareto}

When optimizing for multiple objectives, there is typically no single optimal
solution.  Instead, there is a set of \emph{Pareto-optimal} solutions:

\begin{definition}[Pareto Optimality]\label{def:pareto}
A solution $c_1$ \emph{dominates} $c_2$ (written $c_1 \prec c_2$) if $c_1$ is
at least as good as $c_2$ in all objectives and strictly better in at least one.
A solution is \emph{Pareto-optimal} if no other solution dominates it.
\end{definition}

The set of Pareto-optimal solutions forms the \emph{Pareto front}: a curve in
the objective space representing the best achievable trade-offs.

