\section{Measuring Energy Consumption}\label{sec:energy-measuring}

\subsection{Hardware Power Measurement}\label{sec:energy-hardware}

Energy consumption can be measured at several levels:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Chip-level:} Intel RAPL (Running Average Power Limit) provides
      energy counters for the package, cores, and memory controller.
\item \textbf{Board-level:} External power meters (e.g., Watts Up? Pro) measure
      the total power draw of the system.
\item \textbf{Component-level:} Specialized hardware (e.g., National Instruments
      data acquisition) can measure individual components.
\end{itemize}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Accounting for Counter Wrap in a Compilation Experiment}

Energy counters report accumulated energy in one hardware domain, not the
energy of the code that ran most recently. Compare compiler variants over
the same amount of useful work, and integrate the counter before dividing
by the number of results. In particular, do not interpret a decreasing raw
counter as negative energy.

\textbf{Contract.} Given timestamped readings of a counter with modulus
\(M\) and scale \(u\) joules per tick, report gross joules per completed
item over the measurement interval. Samples include both boundaries and
belong to the same counter and measurement run. Assume no resets, valid
increasing timestamps, and a conservative upper bound \(R\) ticks per
second. Reject intervals in which \(R\Delta t\ge M\), and do not divide
by zero for a run that produced no completed items.

\begin{sudocode}[Integrate a bounded counter]
\If{$m=0$ or fewer than two samples}
  \State \Return no estimate
\EndIf
\Let{e}{0}
\For{each adjacent pair $(t,a),(t',b)$}
  \To{Avoid an ambiguous whole wrap between readings.}
  \If{$R(t'-t)\ge M$}
    \State \Return no estimate
  \EndIf
  \Let{d}{(b-a)\bmod M}
  \If{$d>R(t'-t)$}
    \State \Return no estimate
  \EndIf
  \To{Accumulate ticks before normalizing by completed work.}
  \Let{e}{e+d}
\EndFor
\State \Return $ue/m$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} For \(M=1000\), \(R=500\) ticks per second,
and \(u=0.001\) joule, take one-second-spaced readings \(940,30,110\).
The deltas are \(90,80\), hence a run completing ten items uses
\(0.017\) gross joule per item. If the first pair were two seconds
apart, the estimator would reject it even if the same delta looked
plausible; the sampling guarantee would no longer hold.

After each accepted pair the accumulator equals the actual interval
total so far, because exactly one integer in \([0,M)\) has the
observed modular difference. The procedure uses constant workspace
and linear work. Pinning the domain and recording its scope matter:
a package total includes background activity and omits devices not
covered by that counter. Label the metric as gross and keep idle
subtraction, if used, a separate documented experiment.

\Cref{fig:visual-109} aligns the three raw samples with their
unwrapped values before normalization by completed work.

