\subsection{Software Energy Estimation}\label{sec:energy-software}

When hardware measurement is not available, energy can be estimated using
\emph{software energy models} that map program behavior to energy consumption.

\begin{definition}[Software Energy Model]\label{def:energy-model}
A \emph{software energy model} is a function $E : \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R}$
that estimates the energy consumed by executing a program $P$.  The model is
typically parameterized by features of the program's execution (instruction mix,
cache miss rate, branch prediction accuracy, etc.).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Instruction-Level Energy Models}\label{sec:energy-instruction}

The simplest energy models assign an energy cost to each instruction:
\[
E(P) = \sum_{i \in \mathit{instrs}(P)} e(i)
\]
where $e(i)$ is the energy cost of instruction $i$.  More sophisticated models
account for:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Instruction interactions:} The energy of executing two instructions
      in sequence may differ from the sum of their individual energies.
\item \textbf{Pipeline effects:} Instructions that stall the pipeline consume
      more energy.
\item \textbf{Memory hierarchy:} Cache misses are significantly more expensive
      than cache hits.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Energy Models for Instructions
% ============================================================
\section{Energy Models for Instructions}\label{sec:energy-models}

\subsection{Base Energy Model}\label{sec:energy-base}

A basic instruction-level energy model decomposes energy into components:

\begin{definition}[Instruction Energy Decomposition]\label{def:energy-decomp}
The energy of executing instruction $i$ is:
\[
e(i) = e_{\mathit{base}}(i) + e_{\mathit{src}}(i) + e_{\mathit{dest}}(i) + e_{\mathit{overhead}}
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $e_{\mathit{base}}(i)$ is the base energy of the instruction type.
\item $e_{\mathit{src}}(i)$ accounts for the operand values (bit transitions).
\item $e_{\mathit{dest}}(i)$ accounts for the destination register writes.
\item $e_{\mathit{overhead}}$ accounts for pipeline and control logic.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Cache-Aware Energy Models}\label{sec:energy-cache}

Memory accesses dominate energy consumption in many programs.  A cache-aware
energy model accounts for the energy cost of different levels of the memory
hierarchy:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Typical Energy Costs per Access}\label{tab:energy-costs}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Memory Level} & \textbf{Energy (nJ)} & \textbf{Latency (cycles)} \\
\hline
Register file & 0.1--1 & 0 \\
L1 cache & 1--5 & 3--4 \\
L2 cache & 5--20 & 10--12 \\
L3 cache & 20--50 & 30--40 \\
Main memory & 100--500 & 200--300 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Calibration}\label{sec:energy-calibration}

Energy models must be \emph{calibrated} by measuring the actual energy
consumption of known programs and fitting the model parameters.  This is
typically done using hardware power measurement tools (e.g., Intel RAPL) and
carefully designed calibration benchmarks.

