\section{Energy-Aware Optimization}\label{sec:energy-optimization}

\subsection{Energy-Aware Instruction Selection}\label{sec:energy-isel}

Instruction selection can be guided by energy costs rather than (or in addition
to) execution time.  For example, on ARM processors, the \texttt{MUL} instruction
may consume less energy than a sequence of \texttt{ADD} instructions for certain
multiplications, even if the \texttt{ADD} sequence is faster.

\begin{example}[Energy-Aware Instruction Selection]\label{ex:energy-isel}
Consider computing $x \times 3$.  Two implementations:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \texttt{ADD r0, r0, r0, LSL \#1} (1 instruction)
\item \texttt{MOV r1, \#3; MUL r0, r0, r1} (2 instructions)
\end{enumerate}
The \texttt{ADD} version is faster (1 cycle vs.\ 3 cycles) but may consume more
energy due to the barrel shifter activity.  An energy-aware instruction selector
would choose based on the optimization objective.
\end{example}

\subsection{Energy-Aware Register Allocation}\label{sec:energy-regalloc}

Register allocation affects energy consumption in several ways:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Spill code:} Spilling registers to memory incurs the high energy
      cost of memory accesses.
\item \textbf{Register transitions:} Frequent writes to the same register cause
      bit transitions that consume energy.
\item \textbf{Register file banking:} Some architectures have register banks
      with different energy costs.
\end{itemize}

An energy-aware register allocator minimizes spills (to avoid memory accesses)
and minimizes register transitions (by preferring assignments that cause fewer
bit changes).

\subsection{Energy-Aware Loop Transformations}\label{sec:energy-loop}

Loop transformations can significantly affect energy consumption by changing the
access patterns of the loop:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Loop fusion:} Combining two loops reduces loop overhead and may
      improve data locality.
\item \textbf{Loop fission:} Splitting a loop may reduce register pressure and
      improve cache behavior.
\item \textbf{Loop interchange:} Changing the nesting order of loops can
      transform column-major access into row-major access, dramatically
      improving cache behavior and reducing memory energy.
\item \textbf{Loop tiling:} Partitioning loops into tiles that fit in cache
      reduces the number of cache misses.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Voltage and Frequency Scaling}\label{sec:energy-dvfs}

\emph{Dynamic voltage and frequency scaling} (DVFS) adjusts the processor's
voltage and clock frequency at run time.  Since energy is proportional to
$V^2 \times f$ and performance is proportional to $f$, there is a cubic
relationship between energy and performance:
\[
E \propto V^2 \times f \propto f^3
\]
Running at lower frequency saves energy cubically but increases execution time
linearly.

An energy-aware compiler can insert DVFS hints---instructions that request
frequency changes---at appropriate points in the program.  For example, before a
memory-intensive section (where the CPU is stalled waiting for data), the
compiler can request a lower frequency.

