\section{Thermal-Aware Scheduling}\label{sec:energy-thermal}

\subsection{The Thermal Problem}\label{sec:thermal-problem}

High energy consumption leads to high temperatures, which in turn cause:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Thermal throttling:} The processor reduces frequency to prevent
      overheating, degrading performance.
\item \textbf{Increased leakage:} Higher temperatures increase leakage current,
      which increases energy consumption (a positive feedback loop).
\item \textbf{Reduced reliability:} High temperatures accelerate hardware
      degradation (electromigration, time-dependent dielectric breakdown).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Thermal-Aware Instruction Scheduling}\label{sec:thermal-sched}

Thermal-aware instruction scheduling arranges instructions to minimize peak
temperature while maintaining performance.  The key insight is that different
functional units generate different amounts of heat; by distributing work across
functional units, the scheduler can avoid hot spots.

\begin{definition}[Thermal-Aware Scheduling]\label{def:thermal-sched}
Given a set of instructions $I$ with dependencies $D$ and thermal profiles
$T : I \to \mathbb{R}$ (heat generated by each instruction), thermal-aware
scheduling finds an ordering $\sigma$ of $I$ that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Respects the dependencies $D$.
\item Minimizes the peak temperature $\max_t \mathit{temp}(t)$.
\item Does not increase the total execution time beyond a given bound.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Thermal Simulation}\label{sec:thermal-sim}

Thermal simulation models the temperature of the processor as a function of
time, given a sequence of instructions.  The standard model uses an RC
(resistor-capacitor) equivalent circuit:
\[
C \frac{dT}{dt} = P(t) - \frac{T - T_{\mathit{ambient}}}{R}
\]
where $C$ is the thermal capacitance, $R$ is the thermal resistance, $P(t)$ is
the power at time $t$, and $T$ is the temperature.

