% Trade-offs: Performance vs.\ Energy
% ============================================================
\section{Trade-offs: Performance vs.\ Energy}\label{sec:energy-tradeoffs}

\subsection{The Performance--Energy Frontier}\label{sec:energy-frontier}

The relationship between performance and energy is not monotonic.  Some
optimizations improve both performance and energy (e.g., reducing cache misses);
others improve one at the expense of the other (e.g., loop unrolling may improve
speed but increase instruction fetch energy).

\begin{definition}[Energy-Delay Product]\label{def:edp}
The \emph{energy-delay product} (EDP) is a combined metric:
\[
\mathit{EDP} = E \times D
\]
where $E$ is energy and $D$ is delay (execution time).  Minimizing EDP balances
energy and performance.  The \emph{energy-delay$^2$ product} ($\mathit{ED}^2\mathit{P}$)
gives more weight to performance.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Dark Silicon and the Power Wall}\label{sec:energy-dark-silicon}

The end of Dennard scaling~\cite{esmaeilzadeh2011dark} means that not all
transistors on a chip can be active simultaneously without exceeding the thermal
design power.  This \emph{dark silicon} phenomenon means that energy efficiency
is no longer optional---it is a fundamental constraint on processor design and,
by extension, on compilation.

\begin{insight}
The shift from performance-only optimization to energy-aware optimization
represents a fundamental change in the compiler's objective function.  Where
once the compiler's goal was to minimize execution time, it must now navigate a
multi-dimensional space of trade-offs between time, energy, temperature, and
other metrics.  This shift mirrors the broader transition in computer
architecture from the single-core frequency-scaling era to the multi-core,
heterogeneous, power-constrained era.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Compiler Strategies for Energy Efficiency}\label{sec:energy-strategies}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Code compaction:} Smaller code means fewer instruction fetches,
      reducing energy.
\item \textbf{Data locality optimization:} Better cache behavior reduces
      expensive memory accesses.
\item \textbf{Algorithmic optimization:} Choosing algorithms with fewer
      operations reduces total energy.
\item \textbf{Approximate computing:} Trading accuracy for energy in
      error-tolerant applications.
\item \textbf{Hardware--software co-design:} Exposing hardware energy features
      (power gates, clock gates) to the compiler.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Energy-aware compilation emerged in the late 1990s and early 2000s, driven by
the rise of mobile computing.  Early work by Tiwari et
al.~\cite{tiwari1994energy} developed instruction-level energy models for
embedded processors.  The work of Sinha and
Chandrakasan~\cite{sinha2001jouletrack} introduced JouleTrack, a tool for
tracking energy consumption at the source level.  As mobile devices and data
centers have grown in importance, energy-aware compilation has become an
increasingly active area of research, with modern compilers incorporating
energy-aware optimizations alongside traditional performance optimizations.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:energy-summary}

Energy-aware compilation extends the compiler's optimization objectives beyond
pure performance to include energy consumption, thermal behavior, and their
trade-offs.  As computing devices become increasingly constrained by power and
thermal limits, energy-aware compilation will become an essential capability of
production compilers.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Accurate energy modeling:} Building energy models that are both
      accurate and fast enough for use in the compiler.
\item \textbf{Multi-objective optimization:} Efficiently exploring the
      Pareto front of time--energy trade-offs.
\item \textbf{Hardware--software co-optimization:} Exposing more hardware
      energy features to the compiler.
\item \textbf{Approximate computing:} Integrating quality-energy trade-offs
      into the compilation process.
\end{itemize}
